From 2a1f1770b60fbb933b63583b8696a380d2db017d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Wine user <noreply@localhost>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 10:11:24 +1030
Subject: [PATCH] server: Re-enable FD_WRITE when a successful send leaves the
 socket unwritable.

On Windows a nonblocking send() either queues all of the data or fails with
WSAEWOULDBLOCK, which re-enables FD_WRITE. On Unix a successful send() can fill
the socket buffer, and ntdll hides short writes by queueing the remainder in
the background. In both cases the application then sees the socket as not
writable without ever having got WSAEWOULDBLOCK, and since AFD_POLL_WRITE is
still in reported_events, FD_WRITE is never signalled again.

This makes curl's Windows multi_wait() (select() precheck, then wait for
FD_WRITE with a 1 second cap) stall for a full second every few 64 KiB blocks
during uploads, e.g. Autodesk Fusion cloud saves run at ~100 KB/s.
---
 server/sock.c | 10 ++++++++--
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/server/sock.c b/server/sock.c
index 93505d5..2afa463 100644
--- a/server/sock.c
+++ b/server/sock.c
@@ -3998,9 +3998,15 @@ static void send_socket_completion_callback( void *private )
     struct iosb *iosb = send_req->iosb;
     struct sock *sock = send_req->sock;
 
-    if (iosb->status != STATUS_SUCCESS)
+    /* send() calls only clear and reselect events if unsuccessful.
+     *
+     * However, unlike on Windows, a successful send() may leave the Unix
+     * socket buffer full (or have its remainder queued in the background
+     * after a short write), so that the application subsequently sees the
+     * socket as not writable without ever having got WSAEWOULDBLOCK. Treat
+     * that the same way, or FD_WRITE would never be signalled again. */
+    if (iosb->status != STATUS_SUCCESS || !check_fd_events( sock->fd, POLLOUT ))
     {
-        /* send() calls only clear and reselect events if unsuccessful. */
         sock->pending_events &= ~AFD_POLL_WRITE;
         sock->reported_events &= ~AFD_POLL_WRITE;
         sock_reselect( sock );
